{"text":"MODEL_FEEDBACK_JSON:
{
  "positive_alignment_reasons": [
    "The Breeze feels like a natural follow-up to Old Sock because I want more of that relaxed Clapton blues sound.",
    "Step Back connects with what drew me to People, Hell & Angels: expressive blues guitar. That connection makes it one of the few albums I would choose today."
  ],
  "negative_misalignment_reasons": [
    "Heaven & Earth, The Endless River, and Rattle That Lock lean toward progressive or atmospheric rock, while I currently want a more direct blues feel.",
    "Croz, Plain Spoken, and Cass County fit parts of my listening, but reflective songwriting or country appeal alone does not give me the same immediate interest as my two blues picks."
  ],
  "overgeneralized_signals": [
    "My interest in Hendrix seems to have been stretched into a broader interest in classic rock, even though the blues connection matters more to me today.",
    "Familiar performers associated with guitar music do not all satisfy the same mood; atmospheric rock and blues guitar feel quite different to me."
  ],
  "missing_or_underweighted_signals": [
    "The connection between Old Sock and a Clapton tribute to JJ Cale matters especially because it promises the relaxed blues listening I want right now.",
    "I rarely choose many items in one visit. An album can suit my general taste, like Let Me Get By, without feeling compelling enough to join my two strongest choices."
  ],
  "summary_diagnosis": "You understood my interest in blues and roots music, but extended it too broadly into classic rock without fully capturing my current preference for relaxed blues and expressive blues guitar."
}"}
